screen('web-article', dict(viewport='desktop', title='Editorial article with reading rail', category='website', type='screen', roles=['detail','media'], style=['editorial','minimal'], tags=['article','serif','typography','desktop','reading'],
  desc='A desktop editorial article. A slim masthead with the publication name and a subscribe button tops the page. The article has a category tag, a large serif headline "The quiet interface", a dek, an author byline with read time, and a wide illustration band; the body runs in a 680px reading column with a drop cap and a pull quote set in italic serif with an accent rule. A left rail shows reading progress and share buttons; a right rail lists "In this piece" headings. Built on {words}. Idea: a measured reading column with everything else pushed to the margins.'),
  '''<div style="height:100%;display:flex;flex-direction:column"><div class="row" style="height:64px;padding:0 48px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><b style="font:400 26px var(--serif)">The Margin</b><div class="row g24" style="margin-left:40px;color:var(--muted);font-size:14px"><span>Design</span><span>Engineering</span><span>Essays</span></div><div class="sp"></div><button class="btn btn-sm btn-p">Subscribe</button></div>
  <div class="row" style="flex:1;align-items:flex-start;gap:0;padding:40px 48px;min-height:0">
   <aside class="col g12" style="width:150px;align-items:flex-start"><span class="small">38% read</span><div style="width:4px;height:160px;border-radius:4px;background:var(--surface2);position:relative"><i style="position:absolute;inset:0 0 62% 0;border-radius:4px;background:var(--accent)"></i></div><span class="ico" style="width:34px;height:34px;border-radius:50%">'''+ic('share')+'''</span><span class="ico" style="width:34px;height:34px;border-radius:50%">'''+ic('bookmark')+'''</span></aside>
   <article class="col g16" style="flex:1;max-width:760px;margin:0 auto"><span class="tag" style="align-self:flex-start">Essay</span><h1 style="font:400 64px/1 var(--serif);letter-spacing:-.02em">The quiet <em style="color:var(--accent)">interface.</em></h1><p class="lede" style="font-size:19px">Why the best products feel like they are barely there — and what it takes to design one.</p>
    <div class="row g12"><span class="av s"></span><span class="small"><b style="color:var(--text)">Ana Ruiz</b> · Oct 14, 2026 · 9 min read</span></div>
    <div style="height:150px;border-radius:calc(var(--r) * .8);background:linear-gradient(120deg,var(--accent2),var(--accent));opacity:.85"></div>
    <p style="font-size:17px;line-height:1.7"><span style="float:left;font:400 64px/.8 var(--serif);margin:6px 10px 0 0;color:var(--accent)">Q</span>uiet interfaces are not empty ones. They are the result of hundreds of decisions to remove, defer and subordinate — so that the one thing you came to do is the loudest thing on the screen.</p>
    <blockquote style="margin:6px 0;padding-left:20px;border-left:3px solid var(--accent);font:italic 400 28px/1.3 var(--serif)">“Every element on the screen is asking for attention. Your job is to decide who gets it.”</blockquote></article>
   <aside class="col g8" style="width:200px;font-size:13.5px;color:var(--muted)"><span class="eyebrow">In this piece</span><b style="color:var(--text);font-weight:600">The cost of attention</b><span>Defaults that disappear</span><span>Motion that explains</span><span>When to be loud</span></aside></div></div>''')

